Fig. 2
 shows the effect of the Far contents (0 to 100 wt%) on the visual aspect of the graft polymer latexes. It can be seen that the latex stability without BA was poor in comparison to the latexes with BA and BA/Far mixture. The latexes obtained with 0 to 50 wt% of Far showed a uniform aspect and white color, whereas the latex without BA showed yellow color and higher phase separation, indicating low monomer conversion and poor colloidal stability. In this case, starch fragment radicals (ACS*) are formed by reducing chain ends and hydrogen abstraction in the same reactor (first step). These radicals initiate the polymerization of BA (second step), leading to the formation of amphiphilic oligomers of poly(BA)-g-starch through the self-assembly of the amphiphilic oligomers to form polymer particles stabilized by grafted starch chains (Cheng et al., 2018; Cheng & Zhao, 2017; Gaborieau et al., 2009; Meshram et al., 2009; Rodrigues et al., 2020; Rodrigues et al., 2021). These reactions occur through the cleavage of ACS into fragments of various sizes by α-amylase and the redox system in an acidic medium (pH = 1.5), followed by the polymerization of BA to form amphiphilic graft oligomers (Fig. 3
). These amphiphilic oligomers subsequently self-assemble into pseudo-micelles, which generate polymer particles that are sterically and electrostatically stabilized by grafted starch chains located on the particle-water interface with ζ-potential ranging from −34.6 to −42.2 mV (Table 2
). Nevertheless, when Far was used without BA (Run Far-100), low particle nucleation was observed, as evidenced by low colloidal stability (Fig. 2) and low overall monomer conversion (Fig. 4
).
